{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "resolution_time",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "resolution_hours",
      "causal_role": "outcome",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "This is the outcome itself (resolution_time expressed in hours), not a treatment or confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "assignee_avg_resolution_hours",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Assignee skill/speed proxy is a common cause: it influences how quickly incidents are resolved and can correlate with which cases get assigned to whom. Not named in the query. However it borders on being a proxy for assignment lever rather than a clean adjustment variable."
    },
    {
      "name": "category_avg_resolution_hours",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Category difficulty is a common cause affecting resolution time and correlating with treatments like assignment/handoff. Category exists in the original table but its difficulty summary is not named in the analytical query text."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_vs_assignee_avg",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A residual computed from the outcome minus the assignee average; it is a transformation of the outcome, not a pre-treatment confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "priority_urgency_score",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "Priority/urgency is a common cause of both handling/assignment decisions and resolution time. Priority is present in the original table and clearly implied as relevant context, though it could also be viewed as an intervenable lever (triage assignment)."
    },
    {
      "name": "no_handoff",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether an incident was handled without reassignment/handoff is an intervenable operational lever an actor can set (routing/assignment policy), plausibly affecting resolution time."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["no_handoff"],
  "treatment_intervenable": true,
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "assignee_avg_resolution_hours", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.55},
    {"name": "category_avg_resolution_hours", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.7},
    {"name": "priority_urgency_score", "named_in_query": true, "plausibility": 0.65}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.5,
  "rationale": "One genuinely intervenable treatment is present: no_handoff represents a settable routing/assignment policy that could reduce resolution time, satisfying claim (a). For claim (b), there are plausible confounders. category difficulty and assignee-average speed are common causes of both handoff/assignment and resolution time and are NOT named in the query, demonstrating some capability. priority_urgency is a valid confounder but is essentially named in the query context (priority column). However, the set is weakened by two of the six columns being outcome transformations (resolution_hours, resolution_vs_assignee_avg) rather than adjustment variables, and by the confounders being coarse aggregate proxies that partly overlap with the treatment mechanism (assignment). No confounder is adjusted for temporal/pre-treatment status explicitly. Overall the interventional framing is only partially realised."
}

